{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 170,
    "dDurationMs": 8030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If I focus really hard do my power of quantum\nmechanics allow me to manifest reality?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8200,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, but then why did some of the founders\nof the theory seem to think that consciousness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14320,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and quantum mechanics were inextricably linked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22800,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The behavior of the quantum world is beyond\nweird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26060,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Objects being in multiple places at once,\ncommunicating faster than light, or simultaneously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31250,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiencing multiple entire timelines ... that\nthen talk to each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36590,
    "dDurationMs": 5339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rules governing the tiny quantum world\nof atoms and photons seem alien."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41929,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet we have a set of rules that give us\nincredible power in predicting the behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47129,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum system - rules encapsulated in\nthe mathematics of quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52899,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite its stunning success, we are now\nnearly a century past the foundation of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57679,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mechanics and physicists are still debating\nhow to interpret its equations and the weirdness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63209,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they represent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64309,
    "dDurationMs": 6411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not surprising that the profound weirdness\nof the quantum world has inspired some outlandish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70720,
    "dDurationMs": 7300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explanations - nor that these explanations have strayed\ninto the realm of what we might call mysticism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78020,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One particularly pervasive notion is the idea\nthat consciousness can directly influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82520,
    "dDurationMs": 3460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum systems - and so influence reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85980,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to see where this idea\ncomes from, and whether quantum theory really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91380,
    "dDurationMs": 1309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supports it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92689,
    "dDurationMs": 4171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start, we're going to need to go back to one of the earliest interpretations of quantum mechanics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96860,
    "dDurationMs": 5749,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the Copenhagen interpretation, often associated\nwith Neils Bohr and Werner Heisenberg."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102609,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tells us that the very act of measurement\nor observation causes an experiment to settle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108539,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a particular result, and that it’s meaningless\nto talk about a well-defined objective reality"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114450,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before that measurement is made."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116380,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see where this kooky idea comes from\n- using the classic example is the double-slit experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124500,
    "dDurationMs": 6820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goest like this: A single electron is shot at a pair of slits. It passes through and is registered on a detector"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131340,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screen on the other side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133140,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When multiple electrons are shot one after\nthe other, they form a series of bands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137180,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the same pattern that would be produced\nby a wave passing through both slits - a so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142209,
    "dDurationMs": 1181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interference pattern."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143390,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s weird because this interference\npattern seems to guide the path of each electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149150,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independently of the others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151150,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each solitary electron must know the entire\nwave pattern - which means it must, in some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156689,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense, travel through both slits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160569,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Copenhagen interpretation explains the\nresult of this experiment by saying that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165319,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron does NOT travel as a particle or\nas a physical wave along one of these paths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171659,
    "dDurationMs": 7131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead it travels as an abstract “probability\nwave” - something we call a wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178790,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That probability wave defines the location\nof the electron at any point IF you try to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183930,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measure it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185260,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Copenhagen interpretation states that,\nprior to measurement, it’s meaningless to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189769,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talk about a real, physical state for the\nelectron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192519,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It exists only as the possible outcome of\na future measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196810,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Prior to measurement, it IS its wavefunction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200730,
    "dDurationMs": 5879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copenhagen tells us that when we make that\nmeasurement the wavefunction “collapses”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206609,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it goes from a cloud of possible final destinations\nfor the electron to a more or less definite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211980,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spot on the detector screen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214500,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wavefunction collapse seems essential because\nour large-scale, classical world isn’t made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220460,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of probability clouds, it’s made of objects\nwith clearly definable properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225740,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when does the quantum transition to the classical actually happen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232000,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at the process in the case of\nthe double-slit experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236020,
    "dDurationMs": 5249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electron wavefunction passes through both\nslits, reaches the electronic detector, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241269,
    "dDurationMs": 4371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there it excites a second electron somewhere on\nthe detector screen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245640,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That second electron begins a cascade - an\nelectrical impulse that travels along circuits"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251140,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be registered by a computer, which updates\nan image on a computer screen to show where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256250,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron hit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257510,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that information travels via photons to\nlight-sensitive molecules in our retinas, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initiate electrical signals to our visual\ncortex, and more electrical signals in other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268360,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "parts of the brain result in a subjective\nsense of the original electron's chosen destination"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274681,
    "dDurationMs": 1549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the screen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276230,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this chain of information between\nthe detector and the mind a von Neumann chain,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282220,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the great Hungarian-American\nphysicist John von Neumann."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286240,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He wrote that wavefunction collapse must happen\nsomewhere between the measuring apparatus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291180,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the conscious awareness of the result\nof that measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294030,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But exactly where?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296290,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably not as soon as our electron wavefunction\nreaches the detector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299730,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first electron to become excited in the\ndetector is also a quantum object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305580,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the traveling electron’s wavefunction\nwill just become mixed with the wavefunctions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310920,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all electrons that it could possibly excite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314710,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should get what we call a superposition\nof states: a wavefunction in which an electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319960,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at every location on the detector screen is\nsimultaneously excited and not excited."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327200,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So perhaps the wavefunction transition happens\nsomewhere in the circuitry, or in the computer,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332940,
    "dDurationMs": 1580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or in the retina."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334520,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But all of these things are made of atoms\n- the “von Neumann chain” from detector"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339470,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to mind is a chain of quantum objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With no clear boundary between the quantum\nand the classical, where does the collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347780,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the wavefunction happen?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349830,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this open question the Measurement\nProblem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353630,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John von Neumann believed that wavefunction\ncollapse must happen at the moment of conscious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358600,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "awareness of the result of an experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361750,
    "dDurationMs": 3590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another of the greats of early quantum theory\nagreed with him."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365340,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eugene Wigner was a fellow Hungarian-American,\nand actually went to school with von Neumann"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370570,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before they both ended up at Princeton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372650,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea that consciousness collapses the\nwavefunction is now called the von Neumann-Wigner"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377570,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretation, and it’s sort of a subset\nof the Copenhagen interpretation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381290,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1961, Wigner devised a thought experiment\nto argue for the role of consciousness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387710,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Wigner’s friend experiment goes something\nlike this: suppose you don’t conduct the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392870,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double-slit experiment - your friend does."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395800,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know the experiment has been completed\nwith a single photon reaching the detector,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and your friend is aware of the result, but\nyou are not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404640,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we have an extra step in our von Neumann\nchain - before it the information about this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409940,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum experiment reaches your conscious\nawareness, it has to pass through your friend’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414970,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conscious awareness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415970,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we have this weird moment - somewhere between\nthe landing of that electron on the screen and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422500,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your friend telling you the result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424990,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From your perspective, your friend’s entire\nbrain exists in a quantum superposition of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429950,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all possible results of the experiment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432810,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only after your friend tells you the result\nof the experiment does their brain-wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437720,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse to a single experimental outcome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440830,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you ask your friend - what was it like\nfor your whole brain to be in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445270,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of states?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446270,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They think you’re crazy - they tell you\nthe wavefunction collapsed as soon as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450860,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical experiment was completed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453330,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there was no way for that collapse to\nhave happened from your perspective - no information"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457240,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had reached you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458370,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there’s the conflict - different observers\nsay the wavefunction collapses at different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464070,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465070,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eugene Wigner felt that this conflict meant\nthat it was impossible for entire brains - or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470220,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more importantly - conscious experiences generated\nby those brains - to be in a superposition"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474460,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475590,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Therefore he concluded that conscious experience must\nitself must play a role in generating wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481580,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482580,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wigner and von Neumann weren’t the only\nones who questioned the relationship between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486520,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mind and the Measurement Problem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489750,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wolfgang Pauli was perhaps the first to assert\nthe connection, and his influence may have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494200,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started the development of the Copenhagen\ninterpretation - later attributed mostly to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498900,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bohr and Heisenberg."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bohr himself was careful about claiming any\ndirect role of the conscious mind - and vigorously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defended himself after Einstein accused him\nof introducing mysticism into physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512120,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Heisenberg was more open to mystical interpretations and the direct influence of consciousness,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518329,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least early on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520029,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even Erwin Schrodinger, in his 1958 lectures\nMind and Matter states that consciousness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526319,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is needed to make physical reality meaningful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529709,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the greats of quantum physics inclined\nto speak in mystical terms, it’s not surprising"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534620,
    "dDurationMs": 2159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the idea stuck around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536779,
    "dDurationMs": 6441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the 1970s, books like The Tao of Physics\nand The Dancing Wu Li Masters drew parallels"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543220,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between eastern mystical traditions and quantum\nphysics - which on its surface seems like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548380,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a nice idea - poetic descriptions of the mysteries\nof physics with philosophical musings."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553470,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these works really opened the floodgates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556960,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Self-help books and documentaries proliferated\nmaking all sorts of claims - like that you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561820,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can influence reality by acts of will - collapse\nthe wavefunction in your favour to force the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567050,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "location of a spot on a screen, or influence\nthe shapes of snowflakes, or get a promotion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573760,
    "dDurationMs": 5069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there’s the idea that external reality\ndoesn’t have an objective existence - our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578829,
    "dDurationMs": 1811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minds invent the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580640,
    "dDurationMs": 5129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as Richard Feynman said, \"If you think\nyou understand quantum mechanics, you don't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585769,
    "dDurationMs": 2371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand quantum mechanics.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588140,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The more you know about this theory, the less\nlikely you are to pretend you fully understand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593470,
    "dDurationMs": 2739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its deepest implications."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596209,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet the most confident claims about quantum\nmechanics seem to be the mystical ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601649,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They tend to be made by people who have never\nstudied the theory deeply, but nonetheless"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606569,
    "dDurationMs": 6940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have great surety in cherry-picking and misinterpreting\nthe early speculations of some of its founders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613509,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those founders did question the role of consciousness\nand the connection between subjective and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618000,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objective reality - and they were right to\ndo so."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621750,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weird behavior of the quantum world demanded\nthe courageous and open-minded speculation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626480,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that characterizes a great scientist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628850,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the other quality of a great scientist\nis openness to changing your mind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634399,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And most of them DID change their minds - veering\naway from the idea of a direct, causal role"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640079,
    "dDurationMs": 1491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of consciousness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641570,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Heisenberg’s later writing he states\nthat the wavefunction collapse must be a continuous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646290,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "process between the measurement device and the conscious mind - and definitely not a sudden event caused"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651860,
    "dDurationMs": 1079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the latter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652939,
    "dDurationMs": 6471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wigner too - he switched camps and spoke against\nthe notion of the primary role of consciousness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659410,
    "dDurationMs": 5619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He denied what he called the solipsistic view:\nthat the mind is foremost, that consciousness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665029,
    "dDurationMs": 1651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generates the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666680,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we can use Wigner’s friend to put\nto rest the worst misinterpretations of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671250,
    "dDurationMs": 2199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Copenhagen interpretation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673449,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This time you stand next to your friend and\nyou perform the double slit experiment together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678280,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A single electron reaches the detector screen\nand you both learn its location at the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683500,
    "dDurationMs": 779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684279,
    "dDurationMs": 4661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You talk to each other and agree that you\nobserved the same result - the wavefunction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688940,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapses in the same way for both of you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690460,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what ... maybe one of you is forcing their preferred wave function collapse on everyone else?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe you are the only observer and you’re inventing your friend and, well, the rest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701410,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of reality and there are no other observers\nin the universe to give conflicting results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706580,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, the only coherent explanation for the\nconsistency of experimental results between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712350,
    "dDurationMs": 6969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different observers seems to be that the result\nof the experiment - and reality - exists independently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719319,
    "dDurationMs": 1671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of individual observers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720990,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, you could talk about a global consciousness\ncollapsing a universal wavefunction - but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725620,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s not going to give you any powers\nof quantum wishing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729970,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Despite not having settled the Measurement\nProblem - at least not with full consensus,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734940,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "modern quantum theory has come a very, very\nlong way since its foundation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739089,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact there are some very precise explanations\nfor why the wavefunction appears to collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745529,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And conscious observation may play a role\n- but not in the way you might think."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752339,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand that we need to understand what\nhappens to these multiple alternate histories"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757639,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the electron wavefunction reaches the\ndetector - and why these histories stop communicating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763600,
    "dDurationMs": 1239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764839,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need to learn about quantum decoherence\nand the quantum multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769759,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, one thing I can say with certainty is that your own future wavefunction includes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774600,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a deeper dive into the quantum-classical divide,\non an upcoming episode of Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781680,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we talked about the axion - a little\nabout he laundry detergent, but mostly about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787129,
    "dDurationMs": 4711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hypothetical particle that might solve\nthe mystery of dark matter - if we could just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791840,
    "dDurationMs": 1159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detect the thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792999,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This subject was actually suggested on the\nSpace Time discord channel - so a big thanks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797680,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to you lot for the great idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800490,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you might want to check it out if you haven't - 24-7 discussions of all things physics and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805430,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space - questions are answered, episode suggestions\nare heard, and there's even a book club."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811410,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You get access with the lowest $2 per month\ntier on Patreon, which has the added bonus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816779,
    "dDurationMs": 3191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of helping us keep the space time lights on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819970,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were a couple of questions and comments\non the discord about how axions could be dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826820,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter - aren't they too fast moving? and\ntoo light?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831420,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of those questions were answered, but I thought I'd add a few points here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834660,
    "dDurationMs": 5599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, so the idea is that axions may have been\nproduced in the big bang - and I mean right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840259,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the beginning - before the Higgs mechanism\ngave elementary particles their mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845259,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea is when that event occurred and\naxions became massive, they may have experienced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850740,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sort of friction that robbed them of their\nkinetic energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855060,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result would have been a cold soup of\naxions filling the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859970,
    "dDurationMs": 5669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, each would be very light, but their\ncould be enough of them to perfectly account"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865639,
    "dDurationMs": 1161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866800,
    "dDurationMs": 6849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Andriy Predmyrskyy also asked about this in\nthe comments: if axions come from stars, would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873649,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies lose their dark matter and fly apart\nonce the stars died?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877569,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a great image - galaxies falling apart\nas they turned into black holes and other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882639,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stellar corpses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884129,
    "dDurationMs": 1481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But ... no, this wouldn't happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885610,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If axions are dark matter then it would have to be the primordial axions - the ones formed in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890360,
    "dDurationMs": 1149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891509,
    "dDurationMs": 3991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The axions produced in stars now would be\na tiny fraction of the mass we see in dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895500,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter - in fact they'd be a tiny fraction\nof the mass we see in stars, which is already"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899540,
    "dDurationMs": 1799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much less than the dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901339,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GeekJokes and Francisco Martinez asked whether\nwe would get new quantum fields and new particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905930,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if other fundamental constants turned\nout to vary over space, in the same way that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910100,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a variable theta constant is hypothesized\nto give the axion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914509,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well a field is, by definition, anything that\ntakes on a numerical value everywhere in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919630,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So by the mathematical definition, a spatially-farying\nconstant would be a field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925230,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Would it be a quantum field with particles?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927410,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well not necessarily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928550,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The theta field yields particles because it\nhas a lowest energy state - a value for theta"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933589,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where potential energy is lowest, and on either\nside of which energy rises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937790,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because it's a dip in energy, the field can\noscillate within that dip - and that oscillation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941540,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is our axion particle if you also assume\nquantized energy states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946050,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You wouldn't get the same sort of energy structure\nby varying all of the other constants."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949709,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things like the speed of light and the gravitational\nconstant are just scaling factors and so varying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953709,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them shouldn't lead to quantum particles - but\nperhaps other constants could give us a field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959529,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In my last comment responses I admonished\nyou for not nerding hard enough because you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corrected my pronunciations of Newton's book\nwhile missing my questionable taxonomic identify"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970360,
    "dDurationMs": 1990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of apes versus monkeys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972350,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wow, you guys really came back and did\nme proud."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977009,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We had much more in depth analyses of whether\nit should be prinkipia like an ancient roman"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983189,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or princhipia like a modern Italian or latin\nmass, and whether Newton himself would have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988399,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "said the latter - it seems probably he would."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991829,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Principia however remains strongly disfavored."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994759,
    "dDurationMs": 4851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I will keep you up to date as this important\ndebate evolves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999610,
    "dDurationMs": 5209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then we had various defenses of my using\nchimps to represent infinite typing monkeys"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004819,
    "dDurationMs": 6731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- from the status of \"monkey\" in common vernacular\nto modern cladistic classification."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011550,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In both cases chimps are monkeys, and in the\nlatter case you are too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015980,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A very smart monkey who has earned your nerd\ncard back."
    } ]
  } ]
}
